def replace(i,text):
 p=d.paragraphs[i];pr=deepcopy(p._p.pPr) if p._p.pPr is not None else None
 p.clear();p.add_run(text)
replace(1,'An evolving propagation history, linked signal predictions, and explicit limits of a nonexpanding interpretation')
replace(2,'Research hypothesis • Revised 9 September 2026 • Focused history and dynamical reconstruction')
replace(4,'We investigate temporal redshift in a physically nonexpanding universe, distinguishing an empirical propagation law from its uncompleted microscopic theory. The original 164-group experiment remains preserved: 104 training, 35 validation and 25 final-test objects yield redshift RMSE 412.441 km/s versus 411.934 km/s for the specified expanding reference, an inconclusive difference. The leading candidate now uses a homogeneous propagation index with dn/dt=γnᵖ, n(today)=1 and fixed γ/c=7.7315×10⁻⁵ per million light-years. Stable atomic standards, photon conservation and flat static material geometry imply R=[(1+z)ᵖ−1]/(κp), D_L=(1+z)R, D_A=R and arrival-interval stretching 1+z. On 960 previously inspected Pantheon+ supernovae, fitting p=0.263906 improves χ² from 898.5 to 850.2, compared with 873.0 for curvature alone and 871.6 for a constant offset alone. A history-plus-offset diagnostic gives 840.6; the fixed expanding reference remains lower at 838.5. These are exploratory processed-data fits, not independent validation. The improved history predicts negative redshift drift but leaves the present local index drift unchanged. An exact cavity identity shows that, within homogeneous nondispersive propagation, protecting the cavity/atom ratio across epochs requires observable redshift to track changing material lengths. Leading electromagnetic compensation can realize that tradeoff but does not establish exact atomic protection. A new reconstructed scalar potential sustains the fitted trajectory while conserving homogeneous field-plus-radiation energy. Its restricted curvature is positive, but its background tuning, global energy behavior, full perturbations, atomic coupling and gravitational completion remain unresolved. Earlier screening, curvature, rotation, thermalization and galaxy-dynamics branches are retained as exploratory history and parked. The paper now specifies a testable effective propagation model and a partial energy-conserving reconstruction; it does not claim a completed cosmological theory.')
replace(82,'The flat evolving-history model is the strongest one-parameter temporal candidate tested so far. With the original coefficient and Cepheid calibration retained, p=0.263906 gives χ²=850.2 on the same 960 supernovae, improving the original exponential result of 898.5 and the curvature-only result of 873.0. A constant-offset diagnostic with the history gives 840.6, while the fixed expanding reference remains at 838.5. The redshift-bin trend is substantially reduced, but a brightness offset remains. The original nearby test is inconclusive. None of these results is an independent validation of the new history or a derivation of atomic protection.')
replace(83,'Appendices I–J define the narrowed programme and its current completion boundary. The emitter–propagation–receiver model links color, duration, brightness and angular size; its cavity identity exposes the cost of exact clock protection. A reconstructed potential closes the homogeneous field–radiation energy equations and sustains the fitted history, but has not supplied a healthy complete matter–light–gravity action. Curvature, rotation, recirculation, CMB thermalization and galaxy-rotation unification remain documented but parked. The immediate requirements are independent brightness and angular-distance validation, a direct assessment of secular cavity drift, and a microscopic coupling that either satisfies those measurements or explicitly changes a stated propagation assumption. The theory must not be described as complete until these requirements and stable nonexpanding gravitational dynamics are demonstrated.')
def h(text,new=False):
 q=d.add_paragraph(text,'Heading 1' if new else 'Heading 2')
 if new:q.paragraph_format.page_break_before=True
 return q
def p(text):return d.add_paragraph(text,'Normal')
h('Appendix I. The focused propagation model and observational test',True)
p('This appendix supersedes the exponential law as the leading observational candidate without deleting its historical tests. It separates stipulated local matter behavior from derived signal behavior. All new fits here reuse already inspected data; they are exploratory, not new holdouts. Shared light–gravity propagation remains an explicit assumption rather than a derived nonlinear interaction.')
h('I.1 One history, four linked observables')
p('Let t denote material-clock time and n(t)>0 a homogeneous nondispersive index, normalized to n(t_o)=1. In the fixed-atom branch, freely propagating modes obey ω=ck/n and conserve spatial wave number k. An atom emits at the same reference frequency used by the receiving atom. Therefore S≡1+z=n_o/n_e. A stationary separation obeys R=∫(c/n)dt; differentiating its endpoints gives dt_o/dt_e=S for neighboring emission events. These statements assume adiabatic propagation, static flat geometry and no scattering or absorption.')
p('With dn/dt=γnᵖ and κ=γ/c, integration gives R(z)=[(1+z)ᵖ−1]/(κp). The continuous p=0 limit is R=ln(1+z)/κ. Conserved photons lose energy by S and arrive at a rate reduced by S, giving F=L/(4πR²S²), D_L=SR and D_A=R. Thus D_L=(1+z)D_A for every p. The same rule preserves arrival-duration stretching; it does not introduce a separate duration parameter. If material lengths, emission luminosities or detector response change, these distance laws must be rederived.')
h('I.2 Fixed-coefficient brightness comparison')
p('The fit retains κ=7.7315×10⁻⁵ per million light-years, M=−19.24888454 from Cepheid hosts and the full published STAT+SYS covariance, including propagated calibration uncertainty and cross-correlations. The 960 noncalibrator supernovae have zHD≥0.1. The implementation uses D_L=(1+zHEL)R(zHD), retaining the previously stated conditional observer-frame convention and released standardization assumptions [21–22].')
p('The original flat exponential gives χ²=898.475. One fitted magnitude offset gives 871.552; one negative-curvature parameter gives 873.041; one fitted history exponent gives 850.201 at p=0.263906. The conditional profile interval at Δχ²=1 is 0.226425–0.301224. A two-parameter history-plus-offset diagnostic gives p=0.224240, offset +0.101119 mag and χ²=840.647. The fixed expanding reference gives 838.483 and was not optimized. The new temporal candidate has not outperformed that reference. Uncertainty in κ is not propagated in these conditional profile intervals.')
p('The four correlated mean residuals change from [0.1383, 0.2004, 0.2182, 0.3631] to [0.0862, 0.1081, 0.0628, 0.1144] mag. Their last-minus-first contrast falls from 0.2249 to 0.0283 mag, and the constant-bin diagnostic falls from 36.743 to 6.465; no naive post-fit significance is assigned. Excluding z≥1 gives p=0.27128. Separate lower- and higher-redshift fits around z=0.6 give approximately 0.33, but share calibration and systematic correlations and are not holdouts. The joint optimum includes cross-subset covariance.')
p('At z=1 the fitted history gives R=3.01622 Gpc and D_L=6.03244 Gpc in the common rest-frame idealization, 9.73% farther than the exponential candidate. At z=2 the distance increase is 16.01%. Numerical quadrature verifies the closed distance expression to 4.44×10⁻¹⁶ in the dimensionless test integrals. These numerical checks validate the calculation only.')
h('Appendix I continued. Local clocks and a new forecast',True)
h('I.3 Redshift drift')
p('For stationary source and observer, differentiating S=n_o/n_e and using dt_e/dt_o=1/S gives, today, dz/dt_o=γ[(1+z)^(1−p)−(1+z)]. The exponential candidate p=0 predicts zero drift; p=0.263906 predicts −2.58495×10⁻¹¹ per year at z=1. This is a conditional forecast, not a measurement. Every exponent has dn/dt=γ today because n_o=1, so changing history does not remove the original local drift for unchanged couplings.')
h('I.4 Atom–wave–cavity constraint')
p('Allow a general atomic reference ν_A(t). A photon emitted on that transition has measured S=(n_oν_A,o)/(n_eν_A,e). A cavity with fixed mode number j and instantaneous material length L has ν_C=jc/(2nL). Exact constancy of ν_C/ν_A throughout the trajectory requires nLν_A=constant. Combining these expressions gives S=L_e/L_o. Thus fixed material lengths and exact cavity/atom protection cancel homogeneous redshift in this model. If redshift survives, fixed geometric separations grow in those shrinking rod units. This is an operational statement, not a proof of full equivalence to an expanding cosmology.')
p('A concrete leading-order family uses electromagnetic density ℒ_EM=ε₀nᵃE²/2−B²/(2μ₀n^(2−a)), minimally coupled nonrelativistic charges with m_e,m_N∝nᵇ, fixed mass ratio and fixed Pauli g factors. It yields wave speed c/n, atomic size ∝n^(a−b), optical frequency ∝n^(b−2a), hyperfine frequency ∝n^(2+b−4a) and cavity frequency ∝n^(b−a−1) if its spacer tracks atomic size. Cavity/optical and hyperfine/optical ratios are constant at this order only for a=1. With b=1 rods remain fixed and redshift cancels; with b=2 atomic frequency remains fixed and redshift survives, but rods shrink as 1/n. This is a Coulomb/Pauli toy, not exact protection against relativistic, nuclear, radiative and solid-state corrections.')
p('For fixed atoms and cavity length, d ln(ν_C/ν_A)/dt=−γ today, or −7.7315×10⁻¹¹/year. This is a forecast to confront with secular-drift data. The cited atom–cavity study [28] motivates comparing optical, hyperfine and cavity responses, but its oscillatory limits after drift processing do not directly establish a bound on this secular model. No raw cavity likelihood is fitted here. Exact protection over epochs is stronger than a small derivative measured today; a stationary present-day coupling response would need its own higher-order and past-spectrum tests.')
h('I.5 Scope of the next test')
p('The leading candidate is flat evolving history with fixed coefficients for an independent brightness validation. Angular distances are a separate discriminant because changing p cannot alter D_L=(1+z)D_A under the present assumptions. The next interaction must calculate atomic and cavity responses together. If a universal index fails, a new mode or boundary-dependent response must be explicitly derived; declaring trapped photons exempt is insufficient. These alternatives are not supplied by the present fit. Curvature, rotation and CMB thermalization remain parked.')
h('Appendix J. Energy-conserving background reconstruction',True)
p('We now construct the minimum homogeneous field–radiation dynamics that can sustain the fitted history. This goes beyond prescribing n(t), but remains a reverse-engineered effective model on fixed spatial geometry. It neither derives p and γ from first principles nor supplies the missing atomic and gravitational interactions.')
h('J.1 Equations and energy transfer')
p('Let K>0 be a constant field kinetic coefficient. Write the homogeneous field energy density as Kṅ²/2+V(n). Conserved radiation modes have Hamiltonian H=∑ck/n in units with ℏ=1; for a fixed physical volume their energy density is u=U/n, where U is the conserved mode-energy invariant. Hamiltonian differentiation gives the field equation K n̈+V′(n)=u/n and the radiation equation u̇=−(ṅ/n)u. Multiplication of the first equation by ṅ proves d[Kṅ²/2+V+u]/dt=0. Radiation energy lost to redshift enters the field sector, without assuming an unmodeled sink.')
p('To realize ṅ=γnᵖ, choose V(n)=V₀−(Kγ²/2)n^(2p)−U/n. Then V′=−Kγ²p n^(2p−1)+U/n²; with u=U/n the field equation becomes n̈=γ²p n^(2p−1), exactly the derivative of the target history. Along this trajectory the conserved total energy is V₀. The potential coefficient U is chosen to match the radiation invariant of this particular background; this cancellation is tuning, not a universal mechanism.')
h('J.2 Solution and domain')
p('With n(t_o)=1 and p≠1, n(t)=[1+(1−p)γ(t−t_o)]^(1/(1−p)) while the bracket is positive. For p=0.263906, extrapolation reaches n=0 approximately 17.57 billion years before today. At that boundary radiation energy U/n diverges and the reconstructed potential is singular. This is a breakdown of this effective branch, not a Big Bang derivation or a measured age. It cannot be advertised as an eternal solution without an extension.')
p('The coupled equations were integrated numerically from n=0.3 to n=1.5 for three illustrative radiation strengths U/(Kγ²)=0.001, 0.1 and 1. The maximum deviation from the analytic n(t) was 8.61×10⁻¹¹, the maximum dimensionless energy error was 1.38×10⁻¹⁰, and the conserved photon invariant was recovered within 8.26×10⁻¹¹. These are synthetic consistency tests, not fits to radiation observations. The parameters K and U remain unspecified by the supernova analysis.')
h('Appendix J continued. Stability and completion boundary',True)
h('J.3 What the stability calculation establishes')
p('For variations at fixed homogeneous photon invariant U, the reduced effective potential is W(n)=V(n)+U/n=V₀−Kγ²n^(2p)/2. Its curvature is W″/K=γ²p(1−2p)n^(2p−2), positive for 0<p<1/2. The best-fit p lies in that interval; today the coefficient is 0.124613γ². If an independent positive spatial-gradient term is assumed, the restricted instantaneous mode frequency would be ω²=v_s²k²+W″/K. This avoids a negative instantaneous mass squared in this restricted calculation. It does not establish bounded time-dependent perturbations, coupled radiation stability, or gravitational stability.')
p('The distinction is material: radiation perturbations do not generally keep U fixed locally, photons transport energy between regions, and matter emission or absorption changes the chosen background invariant. Those effects require a phase-space radiation distribution coupled to spatial field equations. Lapse, shift, tensor propagation, gravitational constraints and the stress supporting static geometry are absent. No stability result for them follows from positive W″.')
h('J.4 Why this is not yet a complete theory')
p('The potential is unbounded below on the full positive-n domain: for U>0 it diverges negatively near n=0, and for p>0 the n^(2p) term decreases without bound at large n. A finite-domain effective description may be useful, but a globally bounded completion must change the potential outside the tested interval and recheck its trajectory. Adding such a repair is not justified by the brightness data alone. The present radiation-dependent tuning also needs a physical origin.')
p('The atomic obstruction remains independent of that energy reconstruction. A field potential does not protect optical, hyperfine and cavity responses to the electromagnetic coupling. Exact cavity protection with fixed material rods cancels redshift under the stated propagation assumptions. Allowing shrinking rods or different propagation for free and trapped waves changes the model and requires new derivations. Neither has been silently adopted.')
p('Completed here are the linked kinematic laws, the exploratory fixed-coefficient history comparison, the redshift-drift forecast, the conditional cavity identity and a homogeneous energy-conserving reconstruction of the target trajectory. Not completed are an observationally acceptable microscopic matter coupling, independent distance validation, a globally healthy field theory, shared photon/tensor dynamics and a stable nonexpanding gravitational background. A claim of a completed theory would exceed these results. The narrowed programme remains scientifically testable without claiming those missing results.')
p('Reproducibility. The companion theory_closure.zip includes the focused brightness calculations and input data, the atom–wave–cavity algebra checks, and the new coupled dynamical integration. Earlier appendices retain their original assumptions and should be read as historical candidate tests rather than ingredients all asserted to coexist. No new astronomical dataset was fitted for Appendix J.')
